    "case_id": "T02",
    "family_id": "F-token_embeddings",
    "run": {
      "query": "In the vector/embedding sections you never spoke about tokenIDs.\nOne question: which thing the embedding models convert to vectors. Token or TokenID.\n\nbased on your explaination in this video, seems it converts tokenids to vectors.\nIf yes, I have the following questions:\n1. How it obtains the semantic/contextual meaning\n2. will the tokenid for a term will be same. For eg. term \"bank\" will be used at different places with different meaning. How and what basis it will get the token ids?",
      "route": "ai_tutor",
      "answer": "Yes—**the model receives token IDs and uses them to look up vectors**. But the ID itself does not contain meaning; it is just an index in the model’s vocabulary.\n\nThe usual flow is:\n\n**Text → tokenizer → token IDs → embedding vectors → model layers → contextual representations**\n\nFor an embedding model, the final step often combines the contextual representations into one vector for the whole text.\n\n### 1. How does a token get semantic or contextual meaning?\n\nThe token ID first selects a learned vector from an embedding table. During training, the model adjusts its vectors and other parameters so it can perform its training task.\n\nThen, in a Transformer, attention layers use the surrounding tokens to update each token’s representation. For example:\n\n- “She sat by the **bank** of the river.”\n- “She deposited money at the **bank**.”\n\nIf *bank* is represented by the same token ID in both sentences, its **initial embedding** is the same. But the surrounding words differ, so the Transformer produces different **contextual representations** for *bank*. An embedding model may then use those contextual representations to create different vectors for the two sentences.\n\n### 2. Will “bank” always have the same token ID?\n\n**Usually, if the same tokenizer processes it in the same way.** Token IDs are assigned by a particular tokenizer’s vocabulary, so they can differ between models or tokenizers. Also, tokenization can depend on details such as capitalization, punctuation, and spaces. A tokenizer might represent `bank`, `Bank`, or ` bank` differently—or split a word into multiple subword tokens.\n\nThe key distinction is:\n\n- **Token ID:** a vocabulary lookup key; not a semantic coordinate.\n- **Initial token embedding:** the learned vector looked up using that ID.\n- **Contextual representation:** a vector that can change depending on the surrounding text.\n\nSo, the *same* “bank” token can have the same ID and initial embedding in two sentences, while later model layers give it different contextual representations.",
      "retrieved_context": [
        {
          "id": "S02",
          "title": "Attention Is All You Need",
          "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/1706.03762v7",
          "text": "Transformer encoder outputs feed decoder cross-attention as keys and values; decoder representations supply queries. Masked decoder self-attention prevents access to subsequent positions. Training can process shifted target tokens in parallel, whereas autoregressive inference generates new tokens sequentially. Learned embedding tables convert token IDs to vectors.",
          "source_type": "research_paper",
          "citation_allowed": true,
          "visibility": "public",
          "source_checked_at": "2026-11-22",
