    "case_id": "T03",
    "family_id": "F-semantic_search",
    "run": {
      "query": "Hey, you didn't explain how the queries are converted into vectors and then matched with these documents?",
      "route": "ai_tutor",
      "answer": "You’re right—I skipped the key step. **The query and each document chunk are converted into vectors by an embedding model, then the query vector is compared with the stored document vectors.**\n\n### How it works\n\n1. **Split documents into chunks.**  \n   Long documents are usually divided into smaller passages so retrieval can return the relevant section rather than an entire document.\n\n2. **Embed each chunk.**  \n   The embedding model turns a chunk of text into a list of numbers, such as `[0.12, -0.08, ...]`. These numbers represent the text’s meaning in a vector space. The vectors are usually calculated and stored ahead of time.\n\n3. **Embed the query.**  \n   When someone asks a question, the system sends it through a compatible embedding model to produce a query vector.\n\n   Internally, a tokenizer first converts text into token IDs. **Those IDs are not the semantic vectors**; the model’s layers transform them into representations, which are then combined into a vector for the text. [Tokenizer overview](https://huggingface.co/docs/transformers/main/en/tokenizer_summary)\n\n4. **Find nearby document vectors.**  \n   The system compares the query vector with stored chunk vectors using a measure such as cosine similarity or dot product. It returns the nearest chunks—often the top *k*. An index such as Faiss can speed up this search, especially for large collections. [Semantic search](https://www.sbert.net/examples/sentence_transformer/applications/semantic-search/README.html) · [Faiss indexes](https://github.com/facebookresearch/faiss/wiki/Faiss-indexes)\n\n**Example:** If the query is “How do I reset my password?”, a chunk saying “To regain access, choose ‘Forgot password’ on the sign-in page” may rank highly—even though it doesn’t use the exact words “reset my password.”\n\nThe query and document embeddings must be compatible: they’re typically produced by the same model or by encoders designed to map queries and documents into the same space. Similarity finds *related* passages; it doesn’t guarantee that a passage actually answers the question.",
      "retrieved_context": [
        {
          "id": "S03",
          "title": "Semantic search",
          "url": "https://www.sbert.net/examples/sentence_transformer/applications/semantic-search/README.html",
          "text": "Semantic search embeds documents and queries into a compatible vector space and retrieves nearby document vectors. The query/document encoders and similarity measure must be compatible. Similarity is not proof that a passage answers the question.",
          "source_type": "official_documentation",
          "citation_allowed": true,
          "visibility": "public",
          "source_checked_at": "2026-11-22",
          "access_check": "opened_public_resource",
